  "text": "sław\n.Resolve the problem step by step. First, we need to understand that the expected number of regions created by drawing line segments through a disk is a well-known result in combinatorial geometry. The problem can be broken down into the following steps:\n\n1. Understand the initial configuration: The disk is divided into four quadrants by two perpendicular diameters.\n2. Determine the expected number of regions created by drawing additional line segments.\n\nFor a disk divided into four quadrants, the expected number of regions created by drawing \\( n \\) line segments is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ R(n) = 1 + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\]\n\nwhere \\( R(n) \\) is the number of regions created by \\( n \\) line segments.\n\nFor \\( n = 25 \\):\n\n\\[ R(25) = 1 + \\frac{25 \\times 26}{2} = 1 + 325 = 326 \\]\n\nHowever, we need to account for the fact that each new line segment can intersect with any of the existing segments, creating additional regions. The formula for the expected number of regions created by \\( n \\) line segments in a disk is more complex and involves the concept of the \"expected number of regions\" in a disk divided by the number of line segments.\n\nFor \\( n = 27 \\):\n\n\\[ R(27) = 1 + \\frac{27 \\times 28}{2} = 1 + 378 = 379 \\]\n\nHowever, the correct formula for the expected number of regions created by \\( n \\) line segments in a disk is given by:\n\n\\[ R(n) = 1 + \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} \\]\n\nFor \\( n = 27 \\):\n\n\\[ R(27) = 1 + \\frac{27 \\times 28}{2} = 1 + 378 = 379 \\]\n\nThus, the expected number of regions into which the 27 line segments divide the disk is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{379} \\]",
